+<a name="c-2"></a>
+## [C-2] `setOracle` has no access control — anyone repoints the price oracle
+
+**Severity**: Critical
+**Category**: access-control, oracles
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:91-94`
+
+### Description
+
+```solidity
+function setOracle(PriceOracle newOracle) external {
+    oracle = newOracle;
+    emit OracleUpdated(address(newOracle));
+}
+```
+
+`setBorrowRate` immediately below it carries `onlyOwner`. `setOracle` does not.
+Whatever review pass added the modifier to one missed the other two (this and
+C-3).
+
+The oracle is the sole input to `collateralValueUsd`, `debtValueUsd` and
+therefore `healthFactor`. Control of it is control of who is solvent.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+Two independent ways to profit, both requiring only that the attacker call
+`setOracle` with a contract of their own:
+
+**Drain the USDC float.** Attacker deploys an oracle returning an absurd price
+for wstETH, calls `setOracle`, deposits 1 wei of wstETH, and borrows the market's
+entire USDC balance against it. `_borrow` checks `healthFactor` against the
+attacker's own oracle, so it passes.
+
+**Harvest every position.** Attacker deploys an oracle returning a near-zero
+price for wstETH and a normal price for USDC, calls `setOracle`, then calls
+`liquidateAll`. Every borrower is now underwater. The attacker repays their
+debts at face value and seizes collateral at the attacker-chosen price — the
+`takeUsd * unit / price` term in `_liquidate` means a price of 1 wei-USD hands
+over essentially unbounded token amounts, capped only by each user's balance.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+```solidity
+function setOracle(PriceOracle newOracle) external onlyOwner {
+    if (address(newOracle) == address(0)) revert ZeroAddress();
+    oracle = newOracle;
+    emit OracleUpdated(address(newOracle));
+}
+```
+
+An oracle swap is the single most dangerous parameter change in a lending
+market. Put it behind a timelock so depositors can exit if they disagree.
+
