+<a name="c-3"></a>
+## [C-3] `setLiquidationThreshold` has no access control — anyone liquidates every position
+
+**Severity**: Critical
+**Category**: access-control, defi-lending
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:96-99`
+
+### Description
+
+```solidity
+function setLiquidationThreshold(uint256 bps) external {
+    liquidationThreshold = bps;
+    emit LiquidationThresholdUpdated(bps);
+}
+```
+
+Same omission as C-2. There is also no bounds check — `bps` may be `0` or
+`type(uint256).max`.
+
+`liquidationThreshold` is a direct multiplier in `healthFactor`, so setting it
+low makes healthy positions liquidatable and setting it high makes insolvent
+positions immortal.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+**Forced mass liquidation (profitable).** In one transaction:
+
+1. `setLiquidationThreshold(0)` — now `healthFactor` returns `0` for every account with non-zero debt, since the whole expression is multiplied by `liquidationThreshold`.
+2. `liquidateAll()` — every borrower is swept. For each, the attacker repays `debt` USDC and receives `debt × 1.05` worth of collateral.
+
+The attacker banks the 5% bonus across the entire book, funded by borrowers who
+were never undercollateralized. With a $10M book that is a $500k extraction, and
+the only capital requirement is enough USDC to cover the repayments for the
+duration of one atomic transaction — which a flash loan supplies.
+
+**Griefing the other direction.** `setLiquidationThreshold(type(uint256).max)`
+makes `healthFactor` overflow-revert or return an enormous value for all
+accounts, so no position can ever be liquidated. Bad debt accrues with no
+remedy.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+```solidity
+uint256 internal constant MAX_LIQUIDATION_THRESHOLD = 9_500;
+
+function setLiquidationThreshold(uint256 bps) external onlyOwner {
+    if (bps == 0 || bps > MAX_LIQUIDATION_THRESHOLD) revert InvalidThreshold();
+    accrueInterest();
+    liquidationThreshold = bps;
+    emit LiquidationThresholdUpdated(bps);
+}
+```
+
+Note the `accrueInterest()` call — changing a risk parameter should settle
+interest at the old parameters first, the same way `setBorrowRate` already does.
+
+While you are here: `liquidationBonus` has no setter at all. If that is
+deliberate, fine; if it was meant to exist, it needs the same treatment.
+
