+<a name="c-4"></a>
+## [C-4] `healthFactor` divides before multiplying — the liquidation threshold is not what the README says
+
+**Severity**: Critical
+**Category**: precision-math, defi-lending
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:146-151`
+
+### Description
+
+```solidity
+function healthFactor(address user) public view returns (uint256) {
+    uint256 debtUsd = debtValueUsd(user);
+    if (debtUsd == 0) return type(uint256).max;
+
+    return (collateralValueUsd(user) / debtUsd) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / BPS;
+}
+```
+
+`collateralValueUsd(user) / debtUsd` is evaluated **first**. Both operands are
+1e18-scaled USD, so the quotient is a bare integer — `1`, `2`, `3` — with every
+fractional digit truncated. Everything downstream is built on that truncated
+integer.
+
+This is the most common precision bug in DeFi and it is unusually damaging here
+because the result gates both borrowing and liquidation.
+
+Work out what the code actually enforces. A position is healthy when
+`healthFactor >= WAD`:
+
+```
+ratio × 8000 × 1e18 / 10000 >= 1e18
+ratio × 0.8 >= 1
+ratio >= 1.25
+```
+
+But `ratio` is an integer, so `ratio >= 1.25` means `ratio >= 2`.
+
+**The protocol requires 200% collateralization and liquidates below it** — not
+the 125% that an 8000 bps threshold is supposed to mean. The configured
+`liquidationThreshold` has almost no effect: any value from 5001 to 10000 bps
+produces the identical 200% boundary, because they all round to the same integer
+requirement.
+
+Concrete cases:
+
+| Collateral | Debt | True ratio | True HF (80% LT) | Computed HF | Liquidatable? |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| $12,000 | $6,000 | 200% | 1.60 | 1.60e18 | no |
+| $11,940 | $6,000 | 199% | 1.59 | 0.80e18 | **yes** |
+| $10,000 | $6,000 | 167% | 1.33 | 0.80e18 | **yes** |
+| $7,500 | $6,000 | 125% | 1.00 | 0.80e18 | **yes** |
+
+The 199% row is the one that matters. That user is comfortably
+overcollateralized by every published parameter of this market, and a third
+party can liquidate them for a 5% penalty.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+No special access needed. The attacker just waits.
+
+1. Alice deposits $12,000 of wstETH and borrows the maximum the contract will let her — $6,000 USDC, at which point her computed HF is exactly `1.6e18`.
+2. wstETH ticks down 0.5%. Her collateral is now $11,940; her true health factor is 1.59, nowhere near liquidation.
+3. `collateralValueUsd / debtUsd` = `11940e18 / 6000e18` = `1`. Computed HF = `1 × 8000 × 1e18 / 10000` = `0.8e18` < `WAD`.
+4. Bob calls `liquidate(alice)`. It passes the `healthFactor(user) >= WAD` guard. Bob repays $6,000 USDC and seizes $6,300 of Alice's wstETH.
+
+Alice loses $300 to a 0.5% price move while 199% collateralized. Every borrower
+who takes the maximum loan is in this position permanently, and the truncation
+boundary is public, so this is a bot-farmable stream of penalties rather than a
+one-off.
+
+This also compounds M-1: because there is no close factor, the liquidation is
+for 100% of the debt, not a partial nudge back to health.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+Multiply first, divide once, at the end:
+
+```solidity
+function healthFactor(address user) public view returns (uint256) {
+    uint256 debtUsd = debtValueUsd(user);
+    if (debtUsd == 0) return type(uint256).max;
+
+    return collateralValueUsd(user) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / (BPS * debtUsd);
+}
+```
+
+`collateralValueUsd` is 1e18-scaled USD and `liquidationThreshold` is at most
+`1e4`, so for any realistic book the numerator is far from overflowing uint256.
+If you want belt and braces, use a `mulDiv` that carries the 512-bit
+intermediate.
+
+Please add a test asserting that a position at 130% collateral with an 8000 bps
+threshold is **not** liquidatable. That single assertion fails against the
+current code and passes against the fix.
+
