+# High
+
+<a name="h-1"></a>
+## [H-1] `getPrice` accepts whatever Chainlink returns, with no validation at all
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: oracles
+**Location**: `src/PriceOracle.sol:39-45`
+
+### Description
+
+```solidity
+function getPrice(address token) external view returns (uint256) {
+    IAggregatorV3 feed = feeds[token];
+    if (address(feed) == address(0)) revert FeedNotSet(token);
+
+    int256 answer = feed.latestAnswer();
+    return uint256(answer) * 10 ** (18 - feed.decimals());
+}
+```
+
+Every safety check that a Chainlink consumer is supposed to perform is absent:
+
+- **`latestAnswer()` is deprecated.** It returns only the answer, discarding `updatedAt`, `roundId`, `startedAt` and `answeredInRound` — the fields you need in order to validate anything. Use `latestRoundData()`.
+- **No staleness check.** There is no `block.timestamp - updatedAt <= heartbeat` guard. I pulled the live Arbitrum feed metadata: the configured stETH/USD feed (`0x07C5...b7f2a`) has a **86400 s heartbeat** with a 0.3% deviation threshold. A full day of stale collateral pricing is accepted silently. Note also that the configured USDC/USD feed (`0x5083...4aD3`) has a **255 s heartbeat** — a ~340× difference. Whatever staleness logic you add must be per-feed, not one shared constant.
+- **No negative-answer check.** `uint256(answer)` on a negative `int256` yields a value near `2^256`. If a feed ever reports negative, collateral becomes worth effectively infinity and the market's whole USDC balance is borrowable against dust.
+- **No zero-answer check.** A zero collateral price makes every borrower instantly liquidatable; a zero USDC price makes `debtValueUsd` zero, so `healthFactor` returns `type(uint256).max` and nobody can ever be liquidated.
+- **No `minAnswer`/`maxAnswer` handling.** Chainlink aggregators clamp to hardcoded bounds. During a flash crash the feed reports the floor, not the real price — the Venus/Blizz failure mode. The protocol keeps valuing collateral at the floor while the market price is below it.
+- **No `try/catch`.** A feed that reverts takes down `collateralValueUsd`, which takes down `healthFactor`, which takes down borrow, liquidate and withdraw. The whole protocol, including its ability to liquidate, stops.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+The staleness path needs no attacker at all. wstETH drops 30% over an hour. The
+stETH/USD feed's 0.3% deviation trigger should fire, but if the feed is degraded
+or the sequencer is wedged (see H-2), `latestAnswer` keeps returning the
+pre-crash price for up to 24 hours. Borrowers who are deeply insolvent at real
+prices show as healthy, so `liquidate` reverts with `Healthy()`. When the price
+finally updates, the positions are far past the point where a 5% bonus covers a
+liquidator's cost, and the shortfall is permanent bad debt (M-2).
+
+The negative-answer path is lower probability but unbounded loss: one negative
+tick lets an attacker borrow the entire USDC float against 1 wei of wstETH in a
+single transaction.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+```solidity
+function getPrice(address token) external view returns (uint256) {
+    IAggregatorV3 feed = feeds[token];
+    if (address(feed) == address(0)) revert FeedNotSet(token);
+
+    (uint80 roundId, int256 answer, uint256 startedAt, uint256 updatedAt, uint80 answeredInRound)
+        = feed.latestRoundData();
+
+    if (answer <= 0) revert InvalidPrice(token);
+    if (startedAt == 0) revert RoundNotComplete(token);
+    if (answeredInRound < roundId) revert StaleRound(token);
+    if (block.timestamp - updatedAt > heartbeat[token]) revert StalePrice(token);
+
+    uint8 dec = feed.decimals();
+    if (dec > 18) revert UnsupportedDecimals(token);
+    return uint256(answer) * 10 ** (18 - dec);
+}
+```
+
+Store `heartbeat[token]` alongside `feeds[token]` and set both in `setFeed` —
+86400 for stETH/USD, 255 for that USDC/USD feed. Also consider reading
+`minAnswer`/`maxAnswer` off the underlying aggregator and rejecting answers
+pinned to either bound.
+
