+<a name="h-4"></a>
+## [H-4] `initialize` is unprotected and the contracts cannot be deployed atomically
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: proxies, access-control
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:56-82`; `src/CollateralVault.sol:27-29`
+
+### Description
+
+`initialize` sets `owner`, `oracle`, `vault` and `debtAsset`, and is guarded
+only by the `initialized` flag — it has no access control, so whoever calls it
+first wins.
+
+`MarketProxy`'s constructor delegatecalls `initData`, which would normally make
+initialization atomic with deployment and close the front-running window. But
+the contracts have a circular construction dependency that prevents that:
+
+- `LendingMarket.initialize` needs the `CollateralVault` address.
+- `CollateralVault`'s constructor takes `market_` and stores it as **immutable**, so it needs the `MarketProxy` address.
+- `MarketProxy`'s constructor is where `initialize` would be called.
+
+You cannot satisfy all three in one transaction with `new`. The natural
+resolution is to deploy the proxy with empty `initData`, deploy the vault
+pointing at it, then call `initialize` on the proxy — and that third call sits
+in the public mempool, unprotected.
+
+There is no deploy script in the repository, so I cannot confirm which sequence
+you intend. That is itself worth flagging: deployment ordering here is
+security-critical.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+1. Team deploys `LendingMarket` implementation, then `MarketProxy` with empty `initData`, then `CollateralVault(proxy)`.
+2. Team broadcasts `proxy.initialize(teamOwner, realOracle, realVault, usdc)`.
+3. Attacker front-runs it with `proxy.initialize(attacker, attackerOracle, realVault, usdc)`.
+4. The team's transaction reverts with `AlreadyInitialized()`. The attacker is now `owner`, with an oracle they control, on the proxy the vault has already immutably bound itself to.
+
+The vault cannot be repointed — `market` is immutable — so the only recovery is
+to redeploy everything and migrate.
+
+Fixing C-1/C-2/C-3 does **not** fix this. Those add `onlyOwner`; this attack
+makes the attacker the owner.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+Two changes, both needed:
+
+**Gate the initializer.** Record the deployer in the implementation's
+constructor and require it:
+
+```solidity
+address private immutable deployer;
+
+constructor() {
+    deployer = msg.sender;
+    initialized = true;  // lock the implementation itself, see L-2
+}
+
+function initialize(...) external {
+    if (msg.sender != deployer) revert NotDeployer();
+    if (initialized) revert AlreadyInitialized();
+    ...
+}
+```
+
+Note `deployer` is `immutable`, so it lives in the implementation bytecode and
+reads correctly through `delegatecall`.
+
+**Break the cycle.** Either give `CollateralVault` a mutable `market` set once
+via an `initializeMarket` call restricted to the deployer, or precompute the
+proxy address with CREATE2 and pass it to the vault constructor so the whole
+deployment fits in one atomic factory transaction. The CREATE2 route is cleaner
+because it keeps `market` immutable.
+
+Whichever you choose, write the deploy script, and have it assert the final wiring
+(`vault.market() == address(proxy)`, `market.owner() == multisig`,
+`market.vault() == address(vault)`) before it returns.
+
