+<a name="h-5"></a>
+## [H-5] `CollateralVault.withdraw` transfers before updating state
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: general (reentrancy), erc20
+**Location**: `src/CollateralVault.sol:44-53`
+
+### Description
+
+```solidity
+function withdraw(address token, uint256 amount) external {
+    uint256 balance = balanceOf[msg.sender][token];
+    if (balance < amount) revert InsufficientBalance();
+
+    IERC20(token).transfer(msg.sender, amount);          // external call
+    balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount;      // state update, after
+    ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender);
+
+    emit Withdrawn(msg.sender, token, amount);
+}
+```
+
+Textbook checks-effects-interactions violation, and made worse by the caching:
+`balance` is read into memory *before* the external call, and the final
+assignment is `balance - amount` rather than a decrement. Every reentrant
+invocation reads the same stale `balance` and, on unwind, writes the same
+`balance - amount`. N nested withdrawals net a single decrement.
+
+`deposit` and `seize` in the same file both order their operations correctly,
+which makes this look like an oversight rather than a considered decision.
+
+### Exploitability
+
+**Not exploitable with wstETH at launch.** wstETH is a plain ERC20 with no
+transfer hook, so there is no reentry point today.
+
+It becomes exploitable the moment any callback-capable token is listed, and the
+README commits to exactly that: weETH and ezETH next, then "a path to
+yield-bearing and rebasing collateral". Any ERC777 (which is ERC20-compatible
+and passes every check `listCollateral` performs), any ERC677 `transferAndCall`
+token, or any token whose transfer path touches attacker-controlled code opens
+this up.
+
+Given the market is upgradeable and `listCollateral` is a routine governance
+action, I would not rely on "we will remember this constraint when we list
+token 4".
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+Assume governance has listed a hook-bearing token `T`:
+
+1. Attacker deposits 100 `T`. `balanceOf[attacker][T] == 100`.
+2. Attacker calls `withdraw(T, 100)`.
+3. `balance = 100`. `T.transfer` fires the attacker's receive hook before returning.
+4. In the hook, the attacker calls `withdraw(T, 100)` again. Storage still reads 100, so the `balance < amount` check passes. Another 100 `T` moves out. Repeat until the vault is empty.
+5. Unwinding, each frame writes `balanceOf[attacker][T] = 0`. The health check runs against a zeroed balance and passes because the attacker has no debt.
+
+The vault is drained of `T` — including other users' deposits, since the vault
+pools custody.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+Reorder to CEI and decrement rather than assign:
+
+```solidity
+function withdraw(address token, uint256 amount) external {
+    uint256 balance = balanceOf[msg.sender][token];
+    if (balance < amount) revert InsufficientBalance();
+
+    balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount;      // effects first
+    ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender);    // then validate
+    IERC20(token).safeTransfer(msg.sender, amount);       // interaction last
+
+    emit Withdrawn(msg.sender, token, amount);
+}
+```
+
+Running `requireHealthy` before the transfer is the right order too — it means
+the solvency check is evaluated against committed state, and it cannot be
+re-entered mid-check.
+
+Add a `nonReentrant` guard as defence in depth (M-7), and use `safeTransfer`
+(M-3).
+
