+<a name="h-6"></a>
+## [H-6] `borrowWithSig` has no nonce and no deadline — one signature is reusable forever
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: signatures
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:161-168`
+
+### Description
+
+```solidity
+bytes32 internal constant BORROW_TYPEHASH = keccak256("Borrow(address borrower,uint256 amount)");
+
+function borrowWithSig(address borrower, uint256 amount, uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) external {
+    bytes32 structHash = keccak256(abi.encode(BORROW_TYPEHASH, borrower, amount));
+    bytes32 digest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked("\x19\x01", domainSeparator, structHash));
+    if (ecrecover(digest, v, r, s) != borrower) revert BadSignature();
+
+    _borrow(borrower, amount);
+}
+```
+
+The signed struct is `(borrower, amount)`. There is no nonce and no deadline, so
+the digest for a given borrower and amount is constant forever. Nothing is
+consumed on use. Anyone holding the signature can replay it in every block until
+the contract is upgraded.
+
+The EIP-712 domain is otherwise well-formed — it includes `chainId` and
+`verifyingContract`, so cross-chain and cross-instance replay are covered. The
+gap is same-contract replay, which is the one that matters here.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+Alice signs an authorization to borrow 1,000 USDC and hands it to a relayer.
+
+1. Relayer (or anyone who observes the signature on-chain, since it is public in calldata after first use) calls `borrowWithSig(alice, 1000, v, r, s)`. Alice receives 1,000 USDC and 1,000 USDC of debt. Intended.
+2. The attacker calls it again. And again. Each call adds 1,000 USDC of debt to Alice and pushes 1,000 USDC to Alice's address.
+3. `_borrow` reverts once Alice's computed health factor drops below `WAD`, so the attacker cannot push her straight into insolvency — but they can walk her right up to the boundary.
+4. The attacker stops one call short of the revert, then calls `liquidate(alice)` on the next tick of adverse price movement and collects the 5% bonus.
+
+Alice's collateral is now fully encumbered against debt she authorized once. She
+holds the borrowed USDC, so this is not a direct theft of principal — the loss is
+the liquidation penalty plus the forced leverage and the interest on debt she
+never wanted.
+
+Because of C-4, step 3's boundary is 200% collateralization rather than 125%, and
+step 4's trigger is a sub-1% price move. The two findings compose into a reliable
+extraction.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+Add both a nonce and a deadline, and bind them into the typehash:
+
+```solidity
+bytes32 internal constant BORROW_TYPEHASH =
+    keccak256("Borrow(address borrower,uint256 amount,uint256 nonce,uint256 deadline)");
+
+mapping(address => uint256) public nonces;
+
+function borrowWithSig(
+    address borrower,
+    uint256 amount,
+    uint256 deadline,
+    uint8 v,
+    bytes32 r,
+    bytes32 s
+) external {
+    if (block.timestamp > deadline) revert SignatureExpired();
+
+    bytes32 structHash = keccak256(
+        abi.encode(BORROW_TYPEHASH, borrower, amount, nonces[borrower]++, deadline)
+    );
+    bytes32 digest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked("\x19\x01", _domainSeparator(), structHash));
+
+    address recovered = ecrecover(digest, v, r, s);
+    if (recovered == address(0) || recovered != borrower) revert BadSignature();
+
+    _borrow(borrower, amount);
+}
+```
+
+Increment the nonce **before** `_borrow`, as written above, so a reentrant path
+cannot reuse it. The `recovered == address(0)` check closes M-11 and
+`_domainSeparator()` closes L-3.
+
