+
+<a name="h-7"></a>
+## [H-7] Anyone can grow `borrowers` without bound and permanently brick `liquidateAll`
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: dos
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:197-207`, `190-195`
+
+### Description
+
+```solidity
+function _borrow(address borrower, uint256 amount) internal {
+    accrueInterest();
+
+    if (principalOf[borrower] == 0) borrowers.push(borrower);
+    principalOf[borrower] += amount * WAD / borrowIndex;
+    ...
+}
+```
+
+The push is guarded on `principalOf[borrower] == 0`, but nothing prevents that
+condition being true repeatedly for the same address, and nothing validates
+`amount`.
+
+`borrow(0)` walks straight through: `principalOf` is 0, so the address is
+pushed; `principalOf += 0` leaves it at 0; `debtOf` is 0 so `healthFactor`
+returns `type(uint256).max` and the health check passes; `debtAsset.transfer(borrower, 0)`
+is a no-op that USDC accepts. The caller is appended to `borrowers` and can
+immediately do it again.
+
+Entries are never removed. `_liquidate` sets `principalOf[user] = 0` without
+pruning, and `repay` never prunes either — so even in normal operation a
+liquidated borrower who borrows again is appended a second time.
+
+`liquidateAll` iterates the whole array:
+
+```solidity
+for (uint256 i = 0; i < borrowers.length; i++) {
+    if (healthFactor(borrowers[i]) < WAD) _liquidate(borrowers[i]);
+}
+```
+
+Each iteration calls `healthFactor`, which calls `collateralValueUsd`, which
+loops every listed collateral making an external oracle call and an
+`IERC20.decimals()` call per token. That is thousands of gas per entry even for
+an address with nothing in it.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+1. Attacker writes a loop calling `LendingMarket.borrow(0)`.
+2. Each iteration costs one `SSTORE` for the array push plus the health-check reads.
+3. On Arbitrum, calldata and execution are cheap enough that padding the array to hundreds of thousands of entries costs a trivial sum — this is precisely the case where an array-filling DoS that would be uneconomic on mainnet is affordable on an L2.
+4. `liquidateAll` now exceeds the block gas limit and reverts unconditionally. It can never succeed again, because there is no way to remove entries and no paginated alternative.
+
+The README describes `liquidateAll` as the keeper's response "after a large price
+move" — so the attack disables the protocol's mass-liquidation path exactly when
+it is most needed. Individual `liquidate(user)` calls still work, which caps this
+below Critical, but the keeper infrastructure the team is relying on is gone.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+Three independent fixes; do all of them.
+
+**Reject zero-amount borrows** (see also M-9 on minimum position size):
+
+```solidity
+if (amount == 0) revert ZeroAmount();
+```
+
+**Track membership explicitly** instead of inferring it from `principalOf`:
+
+```solidity
+mapping(address => bool) public isBorrower;
+
+if (!isBorrower[borrower]) {
+    isBorrower[borrower] = true;
+    borrowers.push(borrower);
+}
+```
+
+Remember the README's append-only storage rule — add `isBorrower` at the end of
+the layout, after `domainSeparator`.
+
+**Paginate the sweep** so it can never be gas-bound:
+
+```solidity
+function liquidateRange(uint256 start, uint256 end) external {
+    accrueInterest();
+    if (end > borrowers.length) end = borrowers.length;
+    for (uint256 i = start; i < end; i++) {
+        if (healthFactor(borrowers[i]) < WAD) _liquidate(borrowers[i]);
+    }
+}
+```
+
+Honestly, I would reconsider whether on-chain enumeration is needed at all.
+Keepers can index `Borrowed` events off-chain and call `liquidate(user)` per
+position, which removes the array, this finding, and M-8 together.
+
+---
+
+<a name="h-8"></a>
+## [H-8] Withdrawals are solvency-checked against a stale borrow index
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: defi-lending, general (documentation-code mismatch)
+**Location**: `src/CollateralVault.sol:50`; `src/LendingMarket.sol:153-155`
+
+### Description
+
+The README states:
+
+> `accrueInterest()` runs at the top of every state-changing entry point and
+> advances the index by the elapsed time since the last accrual.
+
+It does not. `borrow`, `repay`, `liquidate`, `liquidateAll` and `setBorrowRate`
+all call it. **`CollateralVault.withdraw` does not** — and withdrawal is the one
+state-changing entry point where an understated debt lets value leave the system.
+
+The path is `withdraw` → `ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender)` →
+`healthFactor` → `debtValueUsd` → `debtOf`:
+
+```solidity
+function debtOf(address user) public view returns (uint256) {
+    return principalOf[user] * borrowIndex / WAD;
+}
+```
+
+`requireHealthy` is declared `external view`, so it structurally *cannot* accrue
+— the fix is not a one-line addition inside it.
+
+Every second since the last accrual is interest the borrower owes but which
+`healthFactor` does not see. The gap is unbounded: `lastAccrualBlock` only moves
+when someone touches a mutating market function, and in a quiet market with no
+supply side (the README says lender deposits land in v2, so there is little
+organic traffic) that can be a long time.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+1. Alice borrows against wstETH, sitting a little above the health boundary.
+2. Nobody calls a market function for a week. `borrowIndex` is a week stale; at 400 bps that is ~0.077% of unrecognised interest.
+3. Alice calls `vault.withdraw(wstETH, x)`, sized so that she is healthy under the stale index and unhealthy under the true one.
+4. The check passes. She keeps the collateral.
+5. The next `accrueInterest()` — anyone's borrow, repay or liquidation — advances the index and Alice's position is immediately underwater.
+
+Alice can also run this deliberately: pick a moment right before a large accrual
+gap closes, or simply repeat small withdrawals, each shaving off the slice of
+collateral the stale index fails to account for.
+
+The single-shot loss is bounded by the accrual gap, which is why this is High and
+not Critical. But it is a direct, repeatable leak of collateral, and it is a
+documented invariant that the code does not hold.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+Make the health check accrue. Change `requireHealthy` from `view` to a mutating
+function that accrues first:
+
+```solidity
+function requireHealthy(address user) external {
+    accrueInterest();
+    if (healthFactor(user) < WAD) revert Unhealthy();
+}
+```
+
+`ILendingMarket.requireHealthy` must drop `view` to match, and `CollateralVault`
+must call it after its own state update (per H-5) so the accrual sees committed
+balances.
+
+Alternatively, have `withdraw` call `market.accrueInterest()` explicitly before
+the check. Either works; the first is harder to forget when someone adds the next
+entry point.
+
+While fixing this, re-read the README claim and make it true — or correct the
+README. A documented invariant that silently does not hold is how the next bug
+gets missed.
+
+---
+
+<a name="h-9"></a>
+## [H-9] `PriceOracle` never validates feed denomination — the next two listings break it
+
+**Severity**: High
+**Category**: oracles
+**Location**: `src/PriceOracle.sol:33-45`; `src/LendingMarket.listCollateral`
+
+### Description
+
+`getPrice` is documented as returning "Price of one whole unit of `token` in USD,
+scaled to 1e18", but `setFeed` accepts any aggregator and `getPrice` performs
+only a decimals adjustment. Nothing checks that the feed is USD-denominated —
+or that it prices the token it is mapped to (that is H-3).
+
+This is about to matter. The README names weETH and ezETH as the next listings. I
+checked what Chainlink actually publishes on Arbitrum for those tokens:
+
+```
+weETH / ETH                0xE141425bc1594b8039De6390db1cDaf4397EA22b   decimals=18
+weETH / eETH Exchange Rate 0x20bAe7e1De9c596f5F7615aeaa1342Ba99294e12   decimals=18
+ezETH / ETH                0x11E1836bFF2ce9d6A5bec9cA79dc998210f3886d   decimals=18
+ezETH / ETH Exchange Rate  0x989a480b6054389075CBCdC385C18CfB6FC08186   decimals=18
+```
+
+There is no `weETH / USD` or `ezETH / USD` aggregator proxy on Arbitrum. The only
+on-chain feeds available for both shortlisted tokens are **ETH-denominated**.
+
+Wire one of those into this oracle and it is accepted silently. `getPrice` reads
+an answer of roughly `1.05e18` — meaning 1.05 ETH — applies `10 ** (18 - 18) == 1`,
+and returns `1.05e18`, which every caller interprets as **$1.05**.
+
+### Proof of Concept
+
+Governance lists weETH with the only feed that exists:
+
+1. `listCollateral(weETH)`; `setFeed(weETH, 0xE141...A22b)`. Both succeed with no warning.
+2. Alice deposits 10 weETH, genuinely worth about $42,000.
+3. `collateralValueUsd(alice)` computes `10e18 × 1.05e18 / 1e18` = `10.5e18` — $10.50.
+4. Alice cannot borrow anything meaningful. Any existing weETH borrower is instantly, irrecoverably liquidatable: reported collateral of $10.50 against real debt guarantees `healthFactor` returns 0.
+5. A liquidator repays the debt and seizes collateral at a price of $1.05/weETH. The `takeUsd * unit / price` term in `_liquidate` converts a trivial USD figure into an enormous token amount, capped only by the user's balance — so the liquidator takes the entire deposit for near-nothing.
+
+Every weETH depositor is wiped out in the first block after listing. The
+symmetric error — a feed that reads high rather than low — would instead let an
+attacker borrow the whole USDC float.
+
+### Recommendation
+
+**Short term**, make listing safe by construction. Record the expected
+denomination and validate it:
+
+```solidity
+function setFeed(address token, IAggregatorV3 feed, uint256 heartbeat_) external onlyOwner {
+    if (address(feed) == address(0)) revert ZeroAddress();
+    if (feed.decimals() > 18) revert UnsupportedDecimals(token);
+    // description() is "STETH / USD", "weETH / ETH", ...
+    require(_endsWith(feed.description(), "/ USD"), "feed not USD-denominated");
+    feeds[token] = feed;
+    heartbeat[token] = heartbeat_;
+    emit FeedUpdated(token, address(feed));
+}
+```
+
+String matching on `description()` is crude; a cleaner version stores an explicit
+`Denomination { USD, ETH }` per feed set by the owner, and has `getPrice`
+multiply ETH-denominated answers through the ETH/USD feed.
+
+**Medium term**, that second form is what you actually need, because it is also
+the fix for H-3 and the only way to support the README's roadmap. A composing
+oracle — `price(token) = feed(token) × rate(token) [× ETH/USD if ETH-denominated]`
+— handles wstETH, weETH and ezETH uniformly. Build it before the next listing,
+not after.
+
+**Also**: `listCollateral` should refuse to list a token that has no feed
+configured (M-4), and listing should be a two-step, timelocked action given how
+much damage a wrong feed does.
+
+---
+
+# Medium
+
+<a name="m-1"></a>
+## [M-1] Liquidation always closes 100% of the debt — no close factor
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: defi-lending
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:209-234`
+
+**Description.** `_liquidate` reads the full `debt`, pulls all of it from the
+liquidator, and zeroes `principalOf[user]`. There is no close factor limiting a
+single liquidation to a fraction (Aave and Compound both cap at 50%).
+
+**Impact.** A borrower one wei below the threshold loses their entire position
+plus a 5% penalty, when repaying a small slice would have restored health. The
+penalty is maximally punitive at the exact moment it needs to be gentlest. Under
+C-4 this triggers at 199% collateralization, and under H-3 the real haircut on
+wstETH is ~26% rather than 5% — the three findings stack on the same user.
+
+**Recommendation.** Cap each liquidation at a configurable close factor:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 public closeFactor; // e.g. 5_000 bps
+
+uint256 maxRepay = debt * closeFactor / BPS;
+uint256 repayAmount = debt < maxRepay ? debt : maxRepay;
+```
+
+Let liquidators pass a `repayAmount` they choose, bounded above by `maxRepay`,
+and recompute health afterwards. Consider allowing a full close only below some
+much lower health factor, where partial liquidation would leave a position too
+small to be worth anyone's gas (M-9).
+
+---
+
+<a name="m-2"></a>
+## [M-2] Liquidator silently absorbs the shortfall; no bad-debt accounting
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: defi-lending
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:209-234`
+
+**Description.** `_liquidate` takes `debt` from the liquidator up front, then
+walks the collateral list seizing up to `seizeUsd`. If the loop ends with
+`seizeUsd > 0` — the position did not hold enough collateral — the function
+completes anyway. The leftover is silently discarded. The liquidator paid full
+debt and received less.
+
+There is no insolvency accounting: no bad-debt counter, no reserve, no event.
+The protocol's books show the debt cleared and nothing records the hole.
+
+**Impact.** Rational liquidators simulate first and refuse unprofitable
+liquidations, so insolvent positions are never cleared and the shortfall becomes
+permanent. Because there is no supply side yet (the README says the treasury
+seeds and takes the interest), the treasury eats it — but the accounts never say
+so, and `liquidateAll` gives no way to distinguish "swept cleanly" from "swept at
+a loss".
+
+This is the Black Thursday shape: a large enough gap between price updates
+(H-1, H-2) and the 5% bonus stops covering the liquidator's cost.
+
+**Recommendation.** Detect and record the shortfall explicitly:
+
+```solidity
+if (seizeUsd > 0) {
+    badDebtUsd += seizeUsd;
+    emit BadDebtRealised(user, seizeUsd);
+}
+```
+
+Only charge the liquidator for what was actually covered, and let the reserve
+absorb the rest. Track `badDebtUsd` as a first-class protocol metric and alert on
+it. Add it at the end of the storage layout.
+
