+<a name="m-3"></a>
+## [M-3] ERC20 return values are never checked
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: erc20
+**Location**: `LendingMarket.sol:177, 204, 213`; `CollateralVault.sol:38, 48, 57`
+
+**Description.** Every token movement in the system is a bare call:
+
+```solidity
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
+debtAsset.transfer(borrower, amount);
+IERC20(token).transfer(to, amount);
+```
+
+Two distinct failure modes:
+
+- **Tokens that return `false` instead of reverting.** The call succeeds, the return value is discarded, and internal accounting credits a transfer that never happened.
+- **Tokens that return nothing.** `IERC20.transfer` is declared `returns (bool)`, so Solidity decodes the empty returndata and reverts. Any such token is unusable as collateral — deposits and withdrawals both fail.
+
+Native USDC on Arbitrum (`0xaf88...5831`, which I confirmed is the correct native
+address, not bridged USDC.e) returns a proper bool, so the debt asset is fine
+today. The exposure is `CollateralVault`, which handles arbitrary listed tokens
+— including the README's USDT shortlist entry, whose return-value behaviour
+differs by deployment and must be verified per chain before listing.
+
+**Recommendation.** Use OpenZeppelin's `SafeERC20` (`safeTransfer`,
+`safeTransferFrom`) everywhere. It handles both cases. Given there are no
+dependencies vendored yet, this is a good moment to add the library rather than
+hand-roll a wrapper.
+
