+
+<a name="m-6"></a>
+## [M-6] Interest accrual uses `block.number` × a hardcoded 12s on a chain where that is the L1 block number
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: chain-specific, precision-math
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:117-126`
+
+**Description.**
+
+```solidity
+uint256 internal constant SECONDS_PER_BLOCK = 12;
+
+uint256 blocksElapsed = block.number - lastAccrualBlock;
+if (blocksElapsed == 0) return;
+uint256 secondsElapsed = blocksElapsed * SECONDS_PER_BLOCK;
+```
+
+On Arbitrum, `block.number` returns an approximation of the **L1** block number,
+not the L2 block number. It advances in jumps (roughly five L1 blocks at a time,
+about once a minute) rather than smoothly.
+
+The long-run average is not badly wrong — L1 blocks are ~12s post-merge, so the
+annualized rate lands close to the intended 400 bps. The problems are:
+
+- **Resolution.** Many L2 transactions share one `block.number`. Any accrual within the same L1 block is skipped entirely (`blocksElapsed == 0`), so interest arrives in lumps.
+- **Fragility.** The correctness of the rate depends on an off-chain constant matching L1 block time forever. If it drifts, so does every borrower's debt, silently.
+- **Portability.** The constant is wrong on every other chain. `SECONDS_PER_BLOCK = 12` on an OP-stack chain (2s blocks) would over-accrue 6×.
+
+Compounding is also discrete-per-accrual rather than continuous, so the effective
+rate depends on how often someone happens to call a mutating function — more
+frequent accruals compound slightly more. Minor, but it means two identical
+positions can owe different amounts.
+
+**Recommendation.** Use `block.timestamp`, which is accurate on Arbitrum and
+needs no constant:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 public lastAccrualTime;
+
+function accrueInterest() public {
+    uint256 elapsed = block.timestamp - lastAccrualTime;
+    if (elapsed == 0) return;
+
+    uint256 growth = borrowIndex * borrowRate * elapsed / (BPS * 365 days);
+    borrowIndex += growth;
+    lastAccrualTime = block.timestamp;
+}
+```
+
+Interest accrual over hours and days is exactly the case where `block.timestamp`
+is appropriate — validator drift of a few seconds is irrelevant at this
+timescale.
+
+Storage note: the README's append-only rule means you cannot repurpose
+`lastAccrualBlock`. Add `lastAccrualTime` at the end of the layout and leave the
+old slot in place.
+
+---
