+<a name="m-7"></a>
+## [M-7] No reentrancy guards anywhere in the system
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: general
+**Location**: `LendingMarket.sol`, `CollateralVault.sol` — all external functions
+
+**Description.** Neither contract has a reentrancy guard. Several functions make
+external calls into token contracts while mid-update:
+
+- `CollateralVault.withdraw` — transfers before writing state (H-5, the acute case).
+- `_liquidate` — calls `vault.seize`, which transfers collateral to `msg.sender`, inside a loop over `collateralTokens`, with `seizeUsd` tracked in memory across iterations.
+- `_borrow` — transfers the borrowed asset to `borrower` after state updates (correctly ordered, but unguarded).
+
+Because `_liquidate` hands control to the liquidator inside the seize loop, a
+liquidator using a hook-bearing collateral token can re-enter `liquidate` for a
+different user, or `repay`, while the outer loop's memory state is live.
+
+**Impact.** With wstETH and USDC there is no hook, so no reentry today. This is
+latent risk that activates on the same trigger as H-5: the first callback-capable
+listing.
+
+**Recommendation.** Add a standard `nonReentrant` guard (OpenZeppelin's
+`ReentrancyGuardUpgradeable` for the market, since it is behind a proxy) to
+`borrow`, `borrowWithSig`, `repay`, `liquidate`, `liquidateAll`, and to the
+vault's `deposit`, `withdraw`, `seize`. Place the modifier first in the chain.
+
+The guard is defence in depth, not a substitute for CEI — fix H-5's ordering
+regardless. Note also that a guard on the market does not protect the vault:
+they are separate contracts sharing state, so both need one.
+
