+<a name="m-8"></a>
+## [M-8] One bad position reverts the entire `liquidateAll` sweep
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: dos
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:190-195`
+
+**Description.** `liquidateAll` calls `_liquidate` in a loop with no per-item
+error isolation. `_liquidate` does `debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), debt)`
+for each position, so the caller must have approved the *sum* of all debts. Any
+single failure — insufficient allowance, insufficient balance, a paused or
+blocklisting token, a seize that reverts — aborts the whole transaction and no
+position is liquidated.
+
+USDC is blocklist-capable, so a single blocklisted borrower's collateral
+transfer can wedge the entire sweep.
+
+**Impact.** The keeper path the README relies on for "after a large price move"
+is fragile precisely when the book is stressed and many positions need clearing.
+Combined with H-7's unbounded array, it is unlikely to work when it matters.
+
+**Recommendation.** Isolate failures per position:
+
+```solidity
+for (uint256 i = start; i < end; i++) {
+    address b = borrowers[i];
+    if (healthFactor(b) >= WAD) continue;
+    try this.liquidate(b) {} catch {
+        emit LiquidationSkipped(b);
+    }
+}
+```
+
+Combine with H-7's pagination. As noted there, off-chain enumeration plus
+per-position `liquidate` calls removes this whole class of problem — each
+liquidation succeeds or fails independently and the keeper retries.
+
