+
+<a name="m-11"></a>
+## [M-11] `ecrecover` return value not checked against `address(0)`
+
+**Severity**: Medium
+**Category**: signatures
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:165`
+
+**Description.**
+
+```solidity
+if (ecrecover(digest, v, r, s) != borrower) revert BadSignature();
+```
+
+`ecrecover` returns `address(0)` for a malformed signature rather than reverting.
+The comparison is against the caller-supplied `borrower`, so passing
+`borrower = address(0)` with garbage `v`, `r`, `s` satisfies the check.
+
+**Impact.** Currently contained: execution reaches `_borrow(address(0), amount)`,
+where `healthFactor(address(0))` computes zero collateral against non-zero debt
+and reverts with `Unhealthy()`. So there is no live exploit — but the only thing
+stopping it is that nobody has deposited collateral to `address(0)` in the vault,
+which nothing prevents. It is one refactor away from being live, and it is free
+to fix.
+
+**Recommendation.** Check explicitly, as shown in the H-6 fix:
+
+```solidity
+address recovered = ecrecover(digest, v, r, s);
+if (recovered == address(0) || recovered != borrower) revert BadSignature();
+```
+
+Better: use OpenZeppelin's `ECDSA.recover`, which reverts on `address(0)` and
+rejects malleable `s` values (L-4) in one call.
+
