+# Low
+
+<a name="l-1"></a>
+## [L-1] Borrow rounding favours the borrower; repay leaves dust principal
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: precision-math
+**Location**: `src/LendingMarket.sol:201, 178`
+
+`_borrow` computes `principalOf[borrower] += amount * WAD / borrowIndex`, which
+floors. Once `borrowIndex > WAD`, a sufficiently small `amount` floors to zero
+principal while the USDC still transfers out — free money, one unit at a time. At
+a borrow index of 1.04 the borrower nets 1 unit (1e-6 USDC) per call, so this is
+not economically meaningful even at Arbitrum gas prices, but the direction is
+wrong: debt should always round **up**, in the protocol's favour.
+
+Symmetrically, `repay` floors the principal reduction, so a full repayment can
+leave a wei of principal behind. `debtOf` then reports 0 while `principalOf` is
+non-zero, which is a semantic trap for any future code that tests
+`principalOf == 0` for closure — including `_borrow`'s own `borrowers` push
+condition (H-7).
+
+**Fix.** Round debt up on entry and down on exit:
+
+```solidity
+principalOf[borrower] += (amount * WAD + borrowIndex - 1) / borrowIndex;
+```
+
+and in `repay`, when `amount == debt`, zero the principal outright rather than
+subtracting a computed figure.
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-2"></a>
+## [L-2] Implementation contract is left uninitialized
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: proxies
+
+`LendingMarket` has no constructor, so the implementation deployed behind
+`MarketProxy` has `initialized == false` and `owner == address(0)`. Anyone can
+call `initialize` directly on the implementation and become its owner.
+
+Impact is limited: the implementation's own storage is never used by the proxy,
+`CollateralVault.seize` is gated on `msg.sender == market` (the proxy address, not
+the implementation), and there is no `selfdestruct` or `delegatecall` in the
+implementation to escalate with. So this is hygiene, not a live hole — but it is
+free to close and it removes a confusing on-chain artifact where a second address
+reports a non-zero `owner`.
+
+**Fix.** Lock it in the constructor (this composes with the H-4 fix):
+
+```solidity
+constructor() {
+    initialized = true;
+}
+```
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-3"></a>
+## [L-3] `domainSeparator` is cached at initialization
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: signatures
+
+`initialize` computes `domainSeparator` once, embedding `block.chainid`. If
+Arbitrum ever forks, the cached value is wrong on one side and signatures either
+stop verifying or become cross-fork replayable.
+
+**Fix.** Store the chain id at init and recompute on mismatch:
+
+```solidity
+function _domainSeparator() internal view returns (bytes32) {
+    return block.chainid == initialChainId ? domainSeparator : _computeDomainSeparator();
+}
+```
+
+Low likelihood, but the fix is a few lines and `borrowWithSig` is being touched
+for H-6 anyway.
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-4"></a>
+## [L-4] Signature malleability in `borrowWithSig`
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: signatures
+
+Raw `ecrecover` accepts both `s` and `n - s` for the same key, so every valid
+signature has a twin that recovers identically. That is harmless today because
+nothing stores or deduplicates signatures — but the H-6 fix does not close it
+either, since nonces key on the borrower rather than the signature.
+
+**Fix.** Reject high-`s` values, or use OpenZeppelin's `ECDSA.recover`, which
+enforces `s <= secp256k1n / 2` and handles M-11 at the same time.
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-5"></a>
+## [L-5] `getPrice` reverts for feeds with more than 18 decimals
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: oracles, precision-math
+
+`10 ** (18 - feed.decimals())` underflows and reverts when `decimals() > 18`. It
+also silently mis-scales if someone assumes 8 everywhere.
+
+Not hypothetical on this chain: Arbitrum carries USDC/USD feeds at both 8
+decimals (`0x5083...4aD3`, the configured one, and `0xfF6B...30F6`) and 18
+decimals (`0x7C7d...A595`, `0x8A35...803d`). The code happens to handle 18
+correctly (`10 ** 0 == 1`), so today's exposure is only to a hypothetical >18
+feed — but the cluster of same-name feeds at differing decimals and heartbeats is
+exactly the configuration hazard that H-1 and H-9 warn about.
+
+**Fix.** Validate in `setFeed` and again in `getPrice`:
+
+```solidity
+uint8 dec = feed.decimals();
+if (dec > 18) revert UnsupportedDecimals(token);
+```
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-6"></a>
+## [L-6] Ownership transfer is single-step in `PriceOracle` and absent in `LendingMarket`
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: access-control
+
+`PriceOracle.transferOwnership` writes the new owner immediately, so a typo
+permanently loses control of the oracle — which, per C-2, is control of the
+market's solvency.
+
+`LendingMarket` is worse: it has an `owner` and an `onlyOwner` modifier but **no
+way to change the owner at all**. Once initialized, the owner is fixed for the
+life of the implementation. Key rotation or a move to a new multisig would
+require a full upgrade.
+
+**Fix.** Use a two-step (`Ownable2Step`) pattern in both: `transferOwnership`
+records a pending owner, `acceptOwnership` from that address commits it. Add the
+missing transfer path to `LendingMarket`, appending `pendingOwner` to the end of
+the storage layout.
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-7"></a>
+## [L-7] No timelock on owner-controlled parameters
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: access-control
+
+Once C-1 through C-3 are fixed, `upgradeTo`, `setOracle`,
+`setLiquidationThreshold` and `setBorrowRate` all take effect in the block they
+are called. Users get no window to exit ahead of a change that alters their
+liquidation price or the code custodying their collateral.
+
+This also means a compromised owner key is an immediate total loss rather than a
+race the community can win.
+
+**Fix.** Route owner functions through a timelock — 48 hours for upgrades and
+oracle changes, shorter for rate changes. Pausing (M-10) should stay immediate;
+that is the point of it.
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-8"></a>
+## [L-8] `collateralValueUsd` makes O(n) external calls per health check
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: dos
+
+`collateralValueUsd` loops every entry in `collateralTokens`, and for each token
+where the user's balance is non-zero it makes two external calls —
+`oracle.getPrice(token)` and `IERC20(token).decimals()`. `healthFactor` calls it,
+so every borrow, withdrawal and liquidation pays this cost, and `liquidateAll`
+pays it once per borrower.
+
+`collateralTokens` only grows — there is no delisting (M-4) — so cost rises
+monotonically with the number of listings and can never be reduced.
+
+**Fix.** Cache `decimals()` at listing time in a `mapping(address => uint8)`
+rather than re-reading it on every call; token decimals are immutable in
+practice. Track a per-user list of tokens actually deposited so the loop skips
+tokens the user has never touched. Both are pure gas wins with no behaviour
+change.
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-9"></a>
+## [L-9] `MarketProxy` accepts ETH that can never be recovered
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: general
+
+`MarketProxy` declares `receive() external payable {}` and a payable `fallback`,
+but `LendingMarket` has no payable functions, no ETH accounting and no sweep. Any
+ETH sent to the proxy — by mistake, or forced in via `selfdestruct` — is stuck
+until an upgrade adds a recovery path.
+
+**Fix.** Drop `receive()` and make the fallback non-payable, since the market has
+no use for ETH. If you would rather keep the door open, add an `onlyOwner` sweep
+to the implementation.
+
+---
+
+<a name="l-10"></a>
+## [L-10] Missing zero-address validation
+
+**Severity**: Low
+**Category**: general
+
+None of the address-setting paths validate their inputs: `initialize` accepts a
+zero `owner_`, `oracle_`, `vault_` or `debtAsset_`; `setOracle` accepts zero;
+`setFeed` accepts zero (which makes `getPrice` revert with the misleading
+`FeedNotSet`); `listCollateral` accepts zero; `CollateralVault`'s constructor
+accepts a zero `market_`, permanently — it is immutable.
+
+Individually minor, but several of these are unrecoverable, and `initialize` is
+already the highest-stakes call in the system (H-4).
+
+**Fix.** Add `if (x == address(0)) revert ZeroAddress();` to each. Cheap
+insurance on a deployment you get one shot at.
+
